From: NeoDarwin
Subject: [PATCH] pfctl: build against xnu's pfvar.h and Darwin's libc

xnu's pf is OpenBSD's of early 2008 (pfvar.h 1.259, pf.c 1.567) with
Apple's changes to the structures pfctl shares with the kernel. pfctl is
compiled against xnu's own <net/pfvar.h>, so every layout is the kernel's;
this patch follows the members Apple renamed:
- a rule's port match is the union xport (TCP/UDP ranges, GRE call IDs,
  ESP SPIs): src.port[]/port_op become src.xport.range.port[]/op, in the
  parser, the printer and the optimizer's field table (which now lists
  the whole union);
- a state carries a family per side of the translation (af_lan, af_gwy:
  NAT64) and the external host as each side sees it (ext_lan, ext_gwy),
  and its ports are xport.port; creation and expiry times are 64-bit.
Darwin: no interface groups (the SIOCGIFGMEMB lookups are left out when
the request doesn't exist), and bison's yacc wants YYSTYPE_IS_DECLARED
beside the parser's own YYSTYPE. base/pfctl/compat/nd_pfctl_compat.h
supplies the rest (SIMPLEQ, the ruleset prototypes, v4/v6).

Rebase-risk: low (OpenBSD 4.3 is fixed; xnu's pfvar.h changes rarely).

--- a/sbin/pfctl/parse.y
+++ b/sbin/pfctl/parse.y
@@ -409,6 +409,7 @@
 	} v;
 	int lineno;
 } YYSTYPE;
+#define YYSTYPE_IS_DECLARED 1	/* bison: the type above, not its default int */
 
